# Test everything and verify it in-game

## HARD RULE — Never run more than 1 subagent at a time

At most ONE subagent (Task tool) may be running at any moment. Do NOT launch
multiple subagents in a single message or in parallel. If you need to explore
several areas, do them SEQUENTIALLY (one Task call, wait for it to return, then
the next) — or, preferred, do the exploration directly yourself with Grep/Glob/
Read. Default to doing the work directly; only use a subagent for a genuinely
large, self-contained investigation, and only one at a time.

## HARD RULE — Do not stop until every PLAN.md task is done

When the user points at `PLAN.md` and asks to "do all tasks", keep working through
**every** unchecked / unverified task in the file, in order, until all are done. Do
NOT stop after a batch or after "a good chunk" — the instruction is to finish the
whole plan. After each task: verify it per the pipeline below, update its
`_STATUS` + checkboxes in `PLAN.md`, commit + push, then move to the next task.
Only stop when literally every `[ ]` box in the actionable task sections is `[x]`
(or explicitly noted as blocked with the reason). If a task is blocked, note the
blocker in `PLAN.md` and move on to the next one — do not stall the whole run.

## HARD RULE — Verify the mechanic with REAL in-game casts, not just direct API calls

When a mechanic is triggered by player/bot actions (damage, abilities, spawning,
pathing), the in-game verification MUST drive it through the **actual game input
path** (bot casting its abilities, a player attacking, a projectile exploding) —
not only by calling the underlying API directly. A dedicated API call proves the
function works; a real cast proves the wiring is connected. Do both where feasible:
isolated deterministic API test + in-game cast test.

A task is only "done" when it has actually run in the game and been confirmed working.
Never report a change as complete on the basis of a code edit, a parse check, a unit
expectation, or a single screenshot that only checks part of the task.

## HARD RULE — verify EVERY requirement of EVERY task

Every task is decomposed into explicit, checkable requirements. **All** of them must be
verified with evidence before the task is marked done:

1. **Enumerate requirements first.** Before touching code, write out each concrete
   requirement of the task (behaviors, edge cases, "on intended hero AND at least one
   other hero", off-cases, visual checks). Treat this list as the acceptance test.
2. **Verify each requirement by simulation + screenshot.** Drive the real game via the
   self-test harness (`powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/selftest/run_selftest.ps1
   -RequestPath tools/selftest/requests/<name>.json`, add `-Hero <id>` when a specific
   hero is the subject; add `--pjotr`/`-Classic` when mode-specific). Read the resulting
   `tools/selftest/results/<name>_report.json` AND open the screenshots with the Read
   tool. A passing report alone is NOT verification — the screenshot must show the
   specific expected outcome.
3. **Assert concrete criteria, not just "no crash."** Encode expected outcomes as
   probe / `expected_*` entries where possible, and visually confirm UI/art changes
   (positions, sizes, colors, text, sprites, motion) in the screenshot.
4. **Cover the failure edges.** Check the off cases too: cooldown still applies after
   release, CPUs are unaffected, non-target heroes don't break, and it works on the
   intended hero *and* at least one other hero.
5. **If it can't be observed yet, build the observation first.** Missing a probe, a
   screenshot hook, a report field, or a test scene means the test environment is
   incomplete — add it so the behavior can be measured, then re-run.
6. **Re-verify previously-completed tasks.** When a later change could affect an earlier
   "done" task (shared code path, shared asset, shared scene), re-run its verification
   before relying on it.
7. **Restart the running app** with `tools/restart_app.ps1` before ending the turn, so
   the user sees the change in the live game.

## Tool suite for screenshot verification (use the RIGHT tool per check)

Four tools live in `tools/`. Pick the one that matches the requirement — never rely
on a single tool:

- `python tools/diff_screenshots.py <before> <after> --out <diff.png> --report <r.json>`
  — "Did anything change?" (per-pixel changed-fraction + bounding box).
- `python tools/inspect_screenshot.py <img.png>`
  — "Does X fill the screen / is it centered / in a corner?" (coverage %, position,
  bounding box + gridded thumbnail).
- `python tools/cv_compare.py <before> <after> --mode all`
  — "Did the object grow/shrink or move?" (SSIM, size-ratio `area_ratio`,
  translation dx/dy, motion heatmap). Best for hero-size / hover-offset / camera checks.
- `python tools/vision_check.py <img(s)> --preset <layout|quality|change|text|size|motion>`
  — LLM (Claude) semantic verdict: reads on-screen text, judges art quality, and names
  *what* changed. Needs `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in `.env` (local, never committed).

Full guidance + preset table: `.cursor/rules/screenshot-analysis.mdc`. The benchmark
that validated these against real before/after pairs: `tools/benchmark_verify.py`.

## No quick passes

Marking a task done without per-requirement evidence is a failure of this rule. If a
requirement cannot be verified, the task stays open (or is explicitly split) — it is not
marked complete.

## When a verification fails

- Fix the root cause, re-run the same test, and re-verify. Do not mark the task done
  while its test is red.
- Keep the failing evidence (report + screenshot path) in your notes so progress is
  auditable.

## HARD RULE — isolated first, then full

Test every mechanic in an ISOLATED context (clean deterministic FFA arena, or a
dedicated empty-world `.tscn`) BEFORE running it in the full / FFA game. Do not
declare a mechanic working on the strength of an FFA/full-scene run alone. If the
mechanic cannot be isolated cleanly, say so explicitly and isolate the parts that
can. Only after the isolated pass is green, re-verify in the full game.

## HARD RULE — multiple screenshots, read ALL of them

Verify with MULTIPLE screenshots covering every distinct phase of the task — never
rely on a single screenshot. Before marking a task done, open EACH captured
`.png` with the Read tool and reason about what is actually shown (not what you
expected). A task is not verified until every screenshot in its batch has been
opened and checked against the acceptance criteria.

## HARD RULE — Isolated tests live in a truly EMPTY world

An "isolated" test scene must contain **ONLY** the mechanic under test and a
camera. Concretely: NO grass, NO ground tiles, NO arena background, NO HUD, NO
world props, NO other objects. A bot/placed entity that triggers the mechanic is
allowed, but NOTHING else may be visible.

- Do NOT instantiate the full `Arena`, do NOT add grass/floor sprites, do NOT load
  the main scene or HUD.
- A flat black/void background (or a single neutral-colored plane if a floor is
  strictly required for the mechanic) is the baseline.
- If an existing "isolated" test renders a full arena background, grass, or HUD,
  it is NOT isolated — fix it to use the empty-world baseline before relying on it.

For ANY visual / cinematic / VFX work (explosions, transitions, hero-ability
presentations, minigames, new worlds), do NOT verify it inside the full main scene.
The main scene's world, obstacles, HUD, and camera are too much noise to judge the
    effect. Instead:

1. **Build an isolated empty-world scene** for the effect (flat/void background + a
   camera, no obstacles, no HUD, no enemies) that plays the effect on its own — e.g.
   `scenes/crash_cinematic_test/crash_cinematic_test.tscn`.
2. **Verify every phase there first** (self-contained screenshots at fixed
   world-times + a `user://selftest_report.json` the scene writes and then
   `get_tree().quit()`). Run it with
   `run_selftest.ps1 -RequestPath ... -Scene res://scenes/<x>/<x>.tscn`.
3. **Only after the isolated world looks correct**, bring the same effect into the
   real world / main scene and re-verify there.

## HARD RULE — Before/After screenshots prove the change landed

Every task that is marked verified/completed must capture the full 6-step
before/after pipeline (see `verification-pipeline.mdc` for details):

| # | Step | File |
|---|------|------|
| 1 | **Isolated BEFORE** (pre-change, isolated world) | `iso_before_*.png` |
| 2 | **Isolated AFTER** (post-change, isolated world) | `iso_after_*.png` |
| 3 | **Isolated COMPARE** (diff or side-by-side; both read) | `diff_iso_*.png` |
| 4 | **In-game BEFORE** (pre-change, real game) | `ingame_before_*.png` |
| 5 | **In-game AFTER** (post-change, real game) | `ingame_after_*.png` |
| 6 | **In-game COMPARE** (diff or side-by-side; both read) | `diff_ingame_*.png` |

- If the change is already made, TEMPORARILY REVERT to capture the BEFORE
  state, then re-apply and capture the AFTER.
- For each COMPARE: use `tools/diff_screenshots.py` (via
  `run_selftest.ps1 -BeforeShot`) or open both images with the Read tool and
  state the concrete pixel differences you see.
- **Read all six** with the Read tool before marking the task done.
- **Order is enforced:** isolated (1–3) must pass before in-game (4–6).
- A task is NOT "verified" without all six on disk.

## HARD RULE — Commit all screenshots to git

For EVERY task that is marked verified/completed:
- All screenshots live under `tools/selftest/results/<feature>_*.png`.
- The selftest report JSON must reference each screenshot path.
- **All result files (PNGs + report JSONs) must be `git add`ed and committed.**
  No files may remain uncommitted in the working tree.
- Run `git add tools/selftest/results/` and commit after every verification batch.
- A task is NOT "verified" without both isolated + in-game screenshots committed
  to git.

## HARD RULE — Isolated test scene MUST be self-contained

When building an isolated test scene:
- It must be a dedicated `.tscn` + `.gd` under `scenes/<name>_test/`.
- It must NOT depend on the full main scene, arena, HUD, or world.
- It must spawn its own actors (dummy players, enemies, etc.) as needed.
- It must write `user://selftest_report.json` with a verdict and screenshot paths.
- It must call `get_tree().quit()` when done.
- Run it via `run_selftest.ps1 -RequestPath ... -Scene res://scenes/<x>/<x>.tscn`.
- The report JSON must include a `verdict` field: "PASS" or "FAIL".

## HARD RULE — Commit + push frequently

After completing any batch of verified tasks:
- `git add -A`
- `git commit -m "descriptive message"`
- `git push`
- **No files may remain saved locally only.** Everything must be pushed to the
  remote repo. If there is no remote, say so explicitly.

## Scope

This applies to all feature work, balance tweaks, quest logic, upgrade paths, boss form,
town quests, corpses, shadows, minimap, ability previews, VFX, and any other system in
this project.
